# Paths a PKGBUILD must never read.
#
# Consulted by aurcache-sandbox when confining the two places makechrootpkg
# executes a PKGBUILD on the worker, outside the chroot. A file rather than an
# environment variable because makechrootpkg reaches the build through
# `sudo --preserve-env=GNUPGHOME,SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, which strips everything else.
#
# Landlock grants are positive only, so these are expanded into "grant every
# sibling on the way down, never the path itself". Everything not listed here
# stays readable, which is the intended trade: the list of secrets is short and
# known, while the set of files a build legitimately reads is neither.
#
# Keep this file readable by the build user, which the sandbox reads it as: it
# lists paths, not secrets, and one that user cannot read refuses every build.

# Build credentials: the SSH key used to fetch authenticated sources, which
# the worker generates and keeps in `ssh/`. Nothing writes `secrets/`, but a
# worker upgraded in place may still hold a copy of a key there.
/var/lib/aurcache-worker/ssh
/var/lib/aurcache-worker/secrets

# The ssh-agent holding that key. Its socket belongs to the build user, as
# every build does, so file permissions cannot keep one build from it; this
# entry can. Where the kernel's Landlock restricts connecting to UNIX sockets
# (ABI 9), a build may connect to nothing beneath a protected path, and a build
# offered the key is granted the agent's socket alone.
/var/lib/aurcache-worker/agent

# The worker's mTLS identity. Stealing it allows impersonating this worker to
# the server, so it is protected here as well as by file ownership.
#
# Named for the files that exist: these were `identity.pem` / `identity.key`,
# which nothing has ever written. `aurcache_worker_core::identity` calls them
# `worker-key.pem` and `worker-cert.pem`, so the list guarded two absent paths
# while the private key it meant to guard was readable.
/var/lib/aurcache-worker/worker-key.pem
/var/lib/aurcache-worker/worker-cert.pem
